1
00:00:07,425 --> 00:00:10,735
Last time, we sorted living things into four groups.

2
00:00:10,955 --> 00:00:13,055
Today, we zoom in on one group.

3
00:00:13,275 --> 00:00:14,125
Animals!

4
00:00:14,188 --> 00:00:15,958
A frog, a bird and a fish.

5
00:00:16,068 --> 00:00:17,328
All at one pond!

6
00:00:17,398 --> 00:00:18,728
Are they one group?

7
00:00:19,151 --> 00:00:20,631
Did you lock in your answer?

8
00:00:20,871 --> 00:00:22,271
One group, or three?

9
00:00:22,521 --> 00:00:23,701
Hold on to it.

10
00:00:24,215 --> 00:00:25,775
It's You against the Buddies.

11
00:00:25,975 --> 00:00:27,275
You lock in first.

12
00:00:27,495 --> 00:00:29,235
One finger for every point.

13
00:00:29,435 --> 00:00:31,725
A little duck is hiding in this video.

14
00:00:31,935 --> 00:00:33,115
Can you spot it?

15
00:00:34,418 --> 00:00:35,368
Round Zero.

16
00:00:35,588 --> 00:00:36,458
A warm-up!

17
00:00:36,728 --> 00:00:38,458
A plant makes its own food.

18
00:00:38,678 --> 00:00:39,718
Can an animal?

19
00:00:39,958 --> 00:00:41,028
One, yes.

20
00:00:41,248 --> 00:00:42,048
Two, no.

21
00:00:42,278 --> 00:00:43,688
Show me with your fingers.

22
00:00:49,160 --> 00:00:50,110
Locked in!

23
00:00:50,375 --> 00:00:51,125
Two!

24
00:00:51,402 --> 00:00:53,932
Animals are unable to make their own food.

25
00:00:54,142 --> 00:00:56,502
They depend on other living things for food.

26
00:00:56,633 --> 00:00:57,623
Two fingers?

27
00:00:57,853 --> 00:00:59,013
One finger up!

28
00:00:59,872 --> 00:01:01,222
Today you'll learn…

29
00:01:01,351 --> 00:01:02,941
What an animal is.

30
00:01:02,994 --> 00:01:05,054
How scientists group animals.

31
00:01:05,101 --> 00:01:07,061
What makes a frog an amphibian.

32
00:01:08,039 --> 00:01:08,959
Are you ready?

33
00:01:09,379 --> 00:01:10,139
Three!

34
00:01:10,529 --> 00:01:11,169
Two!

35
00:01:11,539 --> 00:01:12,169
One!

36
00:01:12,569 --> 00:01:13,239
Go!

37
00:01:13,737 --> 00:01:15,277
So, what is an animal?

38
00:01:15,557 --> 00:01:16,617
Look at the frog.

39
00:01:16,937 --> 00:01:17,687
A head.

40
00:01:17,977 --> 00:01:18,817
A body.

41
00:01:19,117 --> 00:01:20,027
And limbs.

42
00:01:20,307 --> 00:01:21,807
Its legs are its limbs.

43
00:01:22,469 --> 00:01:23,669
Say it with me!

44
00:01:24,381 --> 00:01:28,281
Animals are living things that usually have parts such as a head,

45
00:01:28,031 --> 00:01:29,471
a body and limbs.

46
00:01:32,094 --> 00:01:36,054
Some animals live only on land, while some live only in water.

47
00:01:36,254 --> 00:01:39,984
There are also some animals that can live both on land and

48
00:01:39,734 --> 00:01:40,504
in water.

49
00:01:41,006 --> 00:01:45,256
Animals have different outer coverings as they live in different places.

50
00:01:48,788 --> 00:01:53,398
Scientists have grouped the diversity of animals around us according to their

51
00:01:53,148 --> 00:01:55,018
similarities and differences.

52
00:01:55,153 --> 00:01:57,133
They use things we can see or measure.

53
00:01:57,353 --> 00:01:59,933
These are called observable characteristics.

54
00:02:04,154 --> 00:02:05,024
Round one.

55
00:02:05,284 --> 00:02:06,934
This frog sits on a leaf.

56
00:02:07,164 --> 00:02:08,284
Then, splash!

57
00:02:08,514 --> 00:02:09,454
It swims.

58
00:02:09,674 --> 00:02:11,024
Where can a frog live?

59
00:02:11,304 --> 00:02:12,774
One, only on land.

60
00:02:13,014 --> 00:02:14,454
Two, only in water.

61
00:02:14,704 --> 00:02:15,704
Three, both.

62
00:02:15,924 --> 00:02:17,334
Show me with your fingers.

63
00:02:22,809 --> 00:02:23,759
Locked in!

64
00:02:23,889 --> 00:02:24,689
Two!

65
00:02:24,719 --> 00:02:25,979
It swims so well.

66
00:02:26,139 --> 00:02:27,709
It must live in water.

67
00:02:27,759 --> 00:02:28,609
Three.

68
00:02:28,819 --> 00:02:31,059
I saw it on a leaf, and in the pond.

69
00:02:31,109 --> 00:02:31,829
Three!

70
00:02:31,929 --> 00:02:33,449
It hops, and it swims.

71
00:02:33,945 --> 00:02:34,935
Be the frog!

72
00:02:35,195 --> 00:02:36,165
Shrink down.

73
00:02:36,395 --> 00:02:37,265
Quietly.

74
00:02:39,653 --> 00:02:40,803
You are a frog.

75
00:02:41,153 --> 00:02:42,953
You sit on a leaf, on land.

76
00:02:43,550 --> 00:02:44,970
Your skin is moist.

77
00:02:45,290 --> 00:02:47,010
It stops you from drying out.

78
00:02:47,608 --> 00:02:48,648
Now, jump!

79
00:02:50,255 --> 00:02:51,405
You are under water.

80
00:02:51,845 --> 00:02:54,625
Your strong legs and webbed feet push you along.

81
00:02:55,144 --> 00:02:57,014
Here, you breathe through your skin.

82
00:02:58,224 --> 00:03:01,084
Back on land, you breathe with your lungs as well.

83
00:03:01,677 --> 00:03:02,877
Land and water.

84
00:03:03,257 --> 00:03:04,407
Both are home.

85
00:03:06,813 --> 00:03:08,123
The answer is three.

86
00:03:08,363 --> 00:03:09,123
Both!

87
00:03:09,782 --> 00:03:10,992
Say it with me!

88
00:03:11,707 --> 00:03:15,807
Amphibians are animals that can live both on land and in water.

89
00:03:15,927 --> 00:03:18,207
Amphibians usually have four legs.

90
00:03:22,728 --> 00:03:26,388
Amphibians can breathe through their skin as well as their lungs.

91
00:03:26,522 --> 00:03:30,412
They have protective moist skin to prevent them from drying out when

92
00:03:30,162 --> 00:03:31,252
they are on land.

93
00:03:33,584 --> 00:03:35,764
Amphibians lay their eggs in water.

94
00:03:35,965 --> 00:03:38,915
Frogs, toads and salamanders are amphibians.

95
00:03:39,049 --> 00:03:40,939
So the frog is an amphibian!

96
00:03:42,152 --> 00:03:43,212
Three fingers?

97
00:03:43,432 --> 00:03:44,592
One finger up!

98
00:03:46,343 --> 00:03:47,043
My rule!

99
00:03:46,793 --> 00:03:48,723
If it swims, it is a fish.

100
00:03:48,793 --> 00:03:49,953
A frog swims.

101
00:03:50,013 --> 00:03:51,113
A duck swims.

102
00:03:51,133 --> 00:03:52,293
So they are fish!

103
00:03:52,640 --> 00:03:54,110
Something is not right.

104
00:03:54,320 --> 00:03:56,420
If you can spot it, cross your arms.

105
00:03:56,640 --> 00:03:57,550
Hold on!

106
00:04:03,057 --> 00:04:04,167
Arms crossed?

107
00:04:04,387 --> 00:04:05,527
One finger up!

108
00:04:05,757 --> 00:04:07,167
Many animals swim.

109
00:04:07,387 --> 00:04:09,997
To find the group, look at the outer covering.

110
00:04:10,430 --> 00:04:11,120
Oops.

111
00:04:11,220 --> 00:04:12,870
I must check the cover first!

112
00:04:13,447 --> 00:04:14,837
Show me your score hand!

113
00:04:15,067 --> 00:04:16,077
More than two?

114
00:04:16,307 --> 00:04:17,587
You beat the Buddies.

115
00:04:17,807 --> 00:04:18,867
Three fingers?

116
00:04:19,087 --> 00:04:20,087
High five!

117
00:04:20,884 --> 00:04:22,214
Did you spot the duck?

118
00:04:22,444 --> 00:04:24,294
It was behind the boardwalk rail!

119
00:04:24,514 --> 00:04:25,784
Tell someone at home.

120
00:04:26,570 --> 00:04:28,730
Let's read today's key words together!

121
00:04:28,970 --> 00:04:29,690
Ready?

122
00:04:29,980 --> 00:04:31,090
Outer covering.

123
00:04:32,646 --> 00:04:34,506
Observable characteristics.

124
00:04:36,858 --> 00:04:37,687
Amphibians.

125
00:04:39,333 --> 00:04:40,383
Moist skin.

126
00:04:41,836 --> 00:04:42,736
Well done!

127
00:04:43,784 --> 00:04:45,764
Next: this animal has fins.

128
00:04:45,974 --> 00:04:47,604
But it hops about on the mud!

129
00:04:47,824 --> 00:04:49,644
Is it a fish, or an amphibian?

130
00:04:49,864 --> 00:04:51,344
Find out in Part Two!

131
00:04:53,109 --> 00:04:54,168
Say it with us!

132
00:04:54,968 --> 00:04:55,678
Ready?

133
00:04:55,803 --> 00:04:56,423
One!

134
00:04:56,803 --> 00:04:57,483
two!

135
00:04:57,808 --> 00:04:58,983
Now you know!
